# Oriented constraints across public radii

Let r be the certified odd prime subgroup order, h=(r-1)/2, m>=3 divide h,
and Y=k^m. The existing Fourier extraction identity is

    fplus(b) = m*b^(h-1)*k*F1(b^-m*Y).

For nonzero k, eliminating the same scalar factor at radii a and b gives

    fplus(a)*b^(h-1)*F1(b^-m*Y)
      - fplus(b)*a^(h-1)*F1(a^-m*Y) = 0.

The factors a^(h-1), b^(h-1) are essential. They are not ordinary signs and
must not be replaced by a^h,b^h. This relation is homogeneous under a common
rotation of the candidate scalar, so it defines a constraint on Y. It retains
relative orientation across radii that the separate products fplus*fminus
were discarding. It does not require an additional character observation.

## Safe anchors and zero observations

The implementation establishes an anchor only when both observed oriented
modes are nonzero. The already-imposed product constraint then certifies that
F1 at the anchor is nonzero at every surviving candidate. No target-dependent
inversion or nonzero assumption is introduced. Earlier non-anchor observations
are not retroactively correlated; their existing constraints remain.

When fplus=0, impose F1=0 explicitly. When fminus=0, impose its corresponding
section=0. On the streaming path the reciprocal identity
F_(m-1)(V)=(-1)^h V^(L-1) F1(V^-1) makes the inverse-chart F1 an equivalent
zero constraint because V is nonzero. Both zero observations impose both.
If no product-nonzero observation occurs, there is no cross-radius anchor;
the zero constraints and existing summaries remain the available information.

All these equations are necessary for the true scalar. The component makes
no claim that they encode the complete oriented transcript: nonzero-mode
inequalities are not added except where the existing product proves them.

## Canonical transport and implementation

Each branch retains the anchor F1 polynomial pair in the common first-radius
W chart. Later radii use scale=b^-m/a0^-m, with scale^-1*(W-V) for the inverse
chart. A retained anchor is reduced modulo the current support after filtering.
Canonical intersections cannot introduce a candidate absent from the predecessor.

The powered path reuses its existing sections[1] and sections[m-1]. The
streaming path returns both factors from the existing paired coefficient
stream; at scale1 it constructs the conjugate from the same F1. It does not
perform another coefficient pass. Additional work includes b^(h-1), storing
and copying anchor pairs, conjugate construction, remainder reductions and
canonical intersections. Initial phase-domain expansion remains unchanged.
The section cap is not a total memory cap. Public source costs, replay and
point verification remain charged; route/call changes are not timing claims.

## Exact oracle and controls

For fixture scalar j, apply the predecessor summary constraints, initial
character, and explicit zero-mode constraints. Let a be the first processed
radius with both target modes nonzero. At every processed radius b from a
onward, require

    target_plus(a)*candidate_plus(b)
      = target_plus(b)*candidate_plus(a).

This is the cross-radius polynomial after cancelling the known nonzero
normalizations and fixture scalar j. Group surviving j by j^m to obtain
an independent support polynomial. A separate zero-only oracle isolates
pruning specifically due to cross-radius correlation. Scalar enumeration
appears only in this correctness oracle, not in runtime recovery.

Fixtures retain both initial characters, zero/wrong-sign controls, repeated
radii, odd h/L, L1, streaming and powered capacities, and public point checks.
The predecessor comparison is support inclusion, not equality, because this
component intentionally adds information.

## Sources and next step

Read the exact signed.rs, transport.rs, powered.rs, balanced-image.rs and
balanced-checks.rs sources from feature-balanced-reciprocal-lift at
13f693c7c969ff894c7c63b5c110295d0d5f97e5. The signed-mode and reciprocal
identities were established and checked in the preceding components;
NIST DLMF18.5.1-2 provides the reciprocal polynomial basis. KB was unavailable
in the preceding turn. No novelty or timing claim is made.

Next investigate applying these relative-mode constraints before full phase
domain expansion, or retaining the oriented modes through an implicit
multi-radius constraint representation. A smaller surviving explicit state
alone does not remove the current upfront domain construction.
